  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThe remnant MCV responsible for this morning's heavy rain \nacross central Illinois is shifting into northern Indiana this \nafternoon. In its wake, widely scattered showers and drizzle \npersist within a broad warm air advection pattern. While locally\nheavy downpours remain possible, limited coverage should \nmitigate renewed flooding concerns through late afternoon. \nExpect muggy conditions with temperatures near 80 degrees under \nmostly cloudy skies.\n\nAnother round of showers and thunderstorms is likely (60%+) \ntonight ahead of an approaching cold front and mid-level \nimpulse. Timing favors an arrival into western Illinois around \nand after 10 pm, reaching eastern Illinois and northwest Indiana\nafter midnight. Poor lapse rates and marginal deep-layer shear \nshould limit the severe threat, but very heavy rainfall and \nlocalized flash flooding remain concerns given precipitable \nwater values exceeding 2 inches. While most activity appears \nprogressive, we are monitoring the potential for backbuilding or\ntraining storms, particularly along the southern periphery of \nthe MCS as the low-level jet veers overnight. Central Illinois \nand Indiana are most at risk, encompassing areas saturated by \nthis morning's rainfall. A Flash Flood Watch was considered, but\nwe opted to hold off due to low confidence in the exact \nplacement of training cells. The evening shift will continue to \nmonitor trends to identify high-risk areas.\n\nA cold front will move southeast across the region Sunday, \nmaintaining a 20-30% chance for scattered showers and \nthunderstorms, primarily southeast of I-55. Humidity will be \nslow to recover following the frontal passage; expect warm, \nhumid conditions with highs in the low to mid-80s.\n\nMainly dry weather is expected Monday and Tuesday as surface\nhigh pressure settles over the Mid-Mississippi Valley. Lower\nhumidity and slightly cooler temperatures will provide a brief\nreprieve before another round of convection arrives late\nTuesday. This next system, driven by a cold front and shortwave\ndigging into the Great Lakes, brings a renewed threat of strong\nstorms and heavy rain Tuesday night. Quieter, cooler weather\nreturns Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nKJB",
